  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Fixing one of the three values leaves a two-value target problem on the rest of the array. Ask how that smaller problem becomes cheap when the data is ordered.",
    "Sort the array, loop over the first value, and sweep the suffix from both ends. A sum below the target can only be raised by moving the left pointer inward, and a sum above it can only be lowered by moving the right pointer inward.",
    "Deduplicate at two levels: never reuse the same first value twice, and after a successful hit push both pointers past equal neighbours so a repeated triple is never recorded."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force enumerates all C(n,3) position triples. For every hit it normalises the three values by sorting them, then stores the normalised triple in a hash set so that repeated value triples collapse, and finally sorts the surviving triples lexicographically. The optimal sorts the values once and then fixes the smallest value with a single loop. The remaining question, find two values in the suffix that sum to the negation of the anchor, is answered with two converging pointers. Sorting makes the check monotone: when the current sum is below zero only the left pointer can raise it, and when the sum is above zero only the right pointer can lower it.",
    "why_optimal": "Every triple is identified by its smallest value, so the anchor loop over distinct values is unavoidable, and the inner pair search is linear in the suffix, giving O(n\u00b2) after the O(n log n) sort. The pair sweep removes the third nested loop entirely. Auxiliary space is the O(n) sorted copy; the returned list of triples is not counted. The scan also emits triples in the required lexicographic order for free, because the anchor value and then the left pointer value only ever increase.",
    "pitfalls": "Skipping duplicate anchors with `a[i] == a[i + 1]` instead of `a[i] == a[i - 1]` silently discards valid triples that happen to start with a repeated value. After recording a hit, both pointers must step past runs of equal values, otherwise the same triple is emitted several times. Calling JavaScript `sort()` without a numeric comparator orders numbers as text, which puts 10 before 9 and destroys the monotonicity the pointer sweep depends on. Sorting the caller's array in place instead of a copy mutates the input. An all-zero array must yield exactly one triple, `[0, 0, 0]`, not one per index combination."
  },
